<edge source="iran_gulf_standoff::supply_risk_perception" target="european_institutional_f::policy_uncertainty" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">A sustained rise in perceived oil‑supply risk forces European institutions into ad hoc debates over emergency gas/oil rationing, strategic stock releases, price caps and fiscal support, increasing the unpredictability and timing of policy decisions.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">iran_gulf_standoff</data>
  <data key="d39">european_institutional_f</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_gulf_standoff::supply_risk_perception" target="global_oil_market_shock::product_price_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">A rising market perception of supply risk raises demand for precautionary inventories and drives speculative buying and risk premia in refined-product markets, elevating refined product price levels rapidly.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">iran_gulf_standoff</data>
  <data key="d39">global_oil_market_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_gulf_standoff::supply_risk_perception" target="russia_ukraine_war::diplomatic_leverage" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising market and policymaker perceptions of Gulf supply risk prompt European and Asian governments to seek assured alternative supplies or concessions, increasing Russia's bargaining power in diplomatic forums where energy access and de-escalation are traded off against demands over Ukraine.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">iran_gulf_standoff</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
